## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design practical tests for eventual consistency in offline-first scenarios, including merge behavior, conflict resolution policy, and data integrity under CI.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first and eventual consistency
- Merge policies (last-writer-wins, deterministic merges, or CRDTs)
- Conflict scenarios and data integrity checks
- Deterministic test datasets and network partition simulations
- CI integration, test isolation, and seed data management

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Jest-like test (pseudo)
describe('offline sync', () => {
  test('merges offline edits on reconnect using last-writer-wins', async () => {
    const server = new MockServer();
    const clientA = new Client(server, { offline: true });

    server.createNote({ id: 'n1', content: 'Hello' });

    // offline edit on client A
    clientA.createNoteOffline({ id: 'n1', content: 'Hello world' });

    // reconnect and sync
    await clientA.sync();

    // verify on server
    expect(server.getNote('n1').content).toBe('Hello world');
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend tests for concurrent offline edits on two devices?
- How would you validate no PII leakage during sync and how would you test rollback on failed sync?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design pragmatic, scalable testing strategies for feature flags in distributed systems. It tests understanding of correctness validation, performance benchmarking, observability, and safe rollback mechanisms across multiregion deployments and canary rollouts.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags, canary deployments, and A/B testing
- Multiregion configuration propagation and consistency
- Observability: metrics, dashboards, and distributed logging
- Failure injection and automated rollback
- CI/CD integration for safe continuous delivery

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test scaffold
function testRankingCanary() {
  const config = { region: 'us-east-1', flag: 'new-ranking-v2', percentage: 10 };
  const baselineResults = runQueries(baselineAlgorithm, syntheticDataset);
  const canaryResults = runQueries(newAlgorithm, syntheticDataset);

  assert(latency(canaryResults) < latency(baselineResults) * 1.1);
  assert(errorRate(canaryResults) < 0.01);
  assert(qualityScore(canaryResults) >= qualityScore(baselineResults) * 0.95);
}

function testRollbackOnFailure() {
  mockConfigSourceFailure();
  const result = evaluateFlag('new-ranking-v2');
  assert(result.fallback === true);
  assert(metrics.incremented('flag.rollback.triggered'));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests resilience and reliability fundamentals using real-world API interaction patterns. It evaluates the ability to design concrete tests, leverage mocks effectively, and reason about backoff strategies, idempotency, and rate limits—core skills for building dependable services.

## Key Concepts

- API mocking with deterministic fixtures
- Retry and backoff strategies with configurable caps
- Idempotent retry semantics to ensure safety
- Per-client rate limiting under concurrent scenarios
- CI integration for reproducible, automated testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example scaffold using a mocking library
const nock = require('nock');
const { pricingClient } = require('./pricingClient');

describe('Pricing API Client', () => {
  beforeEach(() => nock.cleanAll());

  test('retries on 429 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(429, 'Too Many Requests', { 'Retry-After': '1' })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 99.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(99.99);
    expect(scope.isDone()).toBe(true);
  });

  test('retries on 500 with exponential backoff', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(500)
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 49.99 });

    const result = await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(result.price).toBe(49.99);
  });

  test('respects max retry cap', async () => {
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .times(4)
      .reply(500);

    await expect(pricingClient.getPrice()).rejects.toThrow('Max retries exceeded');
  });

  test('idempotent retries do not cause side effects', async () => {
    let callCount = 0;
    const scope = nock('https://api.pricing.example')
      .get('/price')
      .reply(() => {
        callCount++;
        return [500, {}];
      })
      .get('/price')
      .reply(200, { price: 100 });

    await pricingClient.getPrice();
    expect(callCount).toBe(1); // First call failed, second succeeded
  });
});
```

